  "query_preview": "..\nThrough next Thursday...\n\nBands of light showers/sprinkles continue along an axis of mid\nlevel frontogenesis from the east side of the Snowy Mtns to far\nsoutheast MT. Some mesonet stations show 0.01\" of rain in the past\n6 hours, and no doubt there has locally been a bit more. Satellite\nimagery shows a shortwave about to clip northeast MT in a WNW flow\naloft. Once this wave passes the frontogenesis will wane and a \nmid level warm front will lift out of our cwa later tonight. The \nrisk of light precip will continue until this happens. As skies \nundergo clearing there could be a low risk of fog tonight but the \nboundary layer remains dry and a shift to downslope gradients is \nexpected tonight in our west. To the east will be a breezy S-SE \nlow level jet. None of the high res models show any hint of fog, \nbut conceptually if there is any potential it would seem to be \nsomewhere in our central cwa from maybe Ingomar to Hardin, \nColstrip & Sheridan. Something to watch.\n\nWe are about to enter an extended period of warm & dry weather\nwhich will confidently last through the end of next week (if not\nlonger). There is more weak energy off the BC coast that will take\na similar track as the current wave. Tomorrow will be warm, dry\nand somewhat breezy in a pre-frontal regime. Another cold front\ndrops out of Canada Friday night, with at most a low chance of\nlight showers in our far east into early Saturday, followed by a \nmodestly cooler Saturday with a backdoor shift to N-E winds. \nStrong western CONUS ridging with above normal heights will \ndominate our weather Sunday through Thursday. The warmest day \nlooks to be Monday when a return to 80s is expected. There is \ngrowing confidence for another weak Canadian front and slight \ncooling for Tuesday. All told, temperatures throughout the period \nwill remain above normal. Early October normal high temps are in \nthe mid-upper 60s.\n\nThe prolonged warmth and dryness will allow for fire concerns to\nincrease again in areas that did not see significant rainfall\nabout 10 days ago. This includes the southern mountains and\nfoothills. Given the occasional weak frontal passage and some\namount of wind this is something to think about.\n\nThe Pacific tropics remain very active. There is a new tropical \nstorm (Choi-Wan) near Guam that is expected to become a typhoon as\nit tracks northward over the coming days. Choi-Wan will \neventually go extratropical in some fashion in the NW Pacific, \npotentially impacting the downstream longwave pattern across the \nnorth Pacific and into western NA. Ensembles show increasing \nspread beginning around Oct 10, hinting at a possible change. This\nis obviously of very low confidence at this time. Stay tuned.\n\nJKL",
